 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# speXtract
     2	
     3	Extraction of pseudo-DDA ("pseudo-MS/MS") spectra from Bruker timsTOF **diaPASEF** data.
     4	
     5	An open, BSD-3, standalone alternative to the closed-source [the reference implementation](https://github.com/Nesvilab/the reference implementation).
     6	The goal is **blind / open search** — finding variants and unexpected modifications that a
     7	library-based DIA tool cannot report, because its search space is enumerated before the search
     8	begins.
     9	
    10	speXtract is a standalone application. **OpenMS is a prerequisite, not a host** — the tool links
    11	against an OpenMS installation but lives outside the OpenMS source tree.
    12	
    13	## Build
    14	
    15	Needs OpenMS with `WITH_OPENTIMS` (to read Bruker `.d`). **Apply this repo's OpenMS patches first:**
    16	
    17	```bash
    18	scripts/apply_openms_patches.sh /path/to/OpenMS      # installs src/TdfMzCalibration.h + patches
    19	# then rebuild the OpenMS DIAPseudoSpectra target
    20	```
    21	
    22	**Why this is not optional.** Without the calibration patch the Bruker reader converts TOF to m/z with
    23	a two-point linear-in-sqrt chord that is **-5 to -11 ppm biased** (m/z dependent) on every file we
    24	measured, costing roughly **6-11% of closed-search peptide identifications**. The build says nothing
    25	about it; the run log names the calibration used, and every emitted mzML records it in the
    26	`spx:mz_calibration` userParam (`tdf_table_modeltype1` | `bruker_sdk` | `legacy_chord_APPROXIMATE`).
    27	An unsupported calibration table now **fails closed** rather than silently producing biased masses;
    28	`SPEXTRACT_ALLOW_CHORD_FALLBACK=1` opts back into the old behaviour.
    29	
    30	Verified against OpenMS `3.6.0-pre-exported-20260717`. Other versions may need the patch rebased.
    31	Optionally, `OPENMS_BRUKER_SDK_PATH=/path/to/libtimsdata.so` uses Bruker's own library instead (an
    32	oracle for cross-checking; not required, not redistributed).
    33	
    34	### Optional: `.mzpeak` input (mzPeak C++ library)
    35	
    36	Build the library once against the same Arrow/Parquet/libzip OpenMS uses (`scripts/build_mzpeak_lib.sh`;
    37	GCC 13 / Arrow 23 need `patches/spx_ranges_to.h` and `patches/mzpeak-cpp-arrow23.py`, both applied by the
    38	script), then configure speXtract with `-DMZPEAK_ROOT=<checkout>`. Inside an OpenMS source tree use
    39	`patches/openms-topp-mzpeak-cmake.py` instead. ims-compact archives store raw TOF behind a two-point
    40	transform; the loader recovers the exact TDF calibration from the embedded `vendor/analysis.tdf.gz` (or
    41	`SPEXTRACT_MZPEAK_TDF=<analysis.tdf.gz>` for `--no-vendor` archives) and refuses to run without it unless
    42	`SPEXTRACT_MZPEAK_EXACT=0`.
    43	
    44	## Run
    45	
    46	```bash
    47	build/DIAPseudoSpectra -in sample.d -out pseudo.mzML -threads 64
    48	```
    49	
    50	Recommended: preload tcmalloc. It is worth more than the structural memory work in this
    51	repository (measured: -18.4% peak RSS combined, of which compaction alone is -6.1%).
    52	
    53	## Where it stands
    54	
    55	Measured on three diaPASEF acquisitions (one cohort, one instrument), both engines, identical search
    56	settings for both tools. Current arm: apex m/z estimator + `charge:min_charge` 2 (2026-09-03).
    57	Full matrix: `docs/BENCHMARK-MATRIX-2026-09-01.md`; decisions: `docs/dataset D-BASELINE.md`.
    58	
    59	| | dataset D | dataset A | dataset B | vs the reference implementation |
    60	|---|---|---|---|---|
    61	| **Sage** peptides @1% FDR | **12,642** | **11,084** | **10,294** | 109.8 / 108.2 / 115.0% |
    62	| Sage, before the charge gate (2026-09-02) | 12,537 | 10,789 | 10,156 | 108.9 / 105.3 / 113.5% |
    63	| **MSFragger** peptidoforms @1% | **12,073** | **12,061** | **9,822** | 86.7 / 88.3 / 92.6% |
    64	| MSFragger, before the charge gate | 11,927 | 11,444 | 9,738 | 85.6 / 83.7 / 91.8% |
    65	| the reference implementation reference (Sage / MSFragger) | 11,517 / 13,932 | 10,242 / 13,666 | 8,948 / 10,606 | -- |
    66	| entrapment FDR at nominal 1% | 1.38% [1.15-1.64] | 0.99% [0.77-1.23] | 1.28% [1.01-1.54] | the reference implementation 1.24 / 1.22% on dataset A/dataset B |
    67	| precursor mass error, paired vs the reference implementation on shared peptides | +0.78 ppm | +1.47 | +1.17 | fragment: -2.4 / -2.0 / -2.1 (the reference implementation-side reporting) |
    68	| pseudo-spectra emitted | 0.66 M | 0.86 M | 0.59 M | **0.94 / 0.99 / 0.82x the reference implementation** |
    69	| wall time, 128-core node (dataset D) | 12:49 | -- | -- | -- |
    70	
    71	Read honestly: **on Sage the result is above parity on all three files; on MSFragger the deficit
    72	stands (7-13%) and is statistically supported.** The emission excess is gone -- since 2026-09-03 we
    73	emit FEWER spectra than the reference implementation on all three files, so per-spectrum efficiency no longer favours
    74	the reference implementation and the MSFragger gap cannot be attributed to over-emission. It also survives perfect
    75	masses, so it is search/detection-side, not calibration.
    76	
    77	The 2026-09-01 calibration fix is worth +6-11% closed-search peptides on Sage. It is an
    78	identification-*yield* effect, not better spectra (emission moved -0.2%): mass error is a feature of
    79	Sage's discriminant. Since the reference implementation's spectra always carried vendor-calibrated masses, every earlier
    80	comparison was biased against the open path -- **this is the removal of a self-inflicted handicap,
    81	not a lead.**
    82	
    83	Determinism: the spectrum data is **byte-identical at any thread count and any loader batch size**
    84	(verified 2026-09-02 on three independent pairs: threads 8 vs 100 twice, loader batch 64 vs 256;
    85	`bench/semantic_digest.py` hashes `<spectrumList>`..`</spectrumList>`, excluding the wall-clock
    86	stamp, the recorded parameters and the trailing byte-offset index, none of which can match across
    87	invocations -- the last of these had produced two false "differs" verdicts before it was excluded).
    88	Last-ulp *code* changes still cascade to ~2% in peptide counts, so compare builds by the accepted
    89	peptide SET and the ppm median, not by raw counts.
    90	
    91	Runtime (dataset D, 33,553 frames, 100 threads on a 128-core node): **15:21 wall, 106 GB peak RSS**
    92	(2026-09-02; was 33:05 on 09-01 -- the serial floor was the MS1 path, now parallel). The loader is
    93	4% of the run; the window loop is 83%. Reading from `.mzpeak` instead of `.d` is supported
    94	(build-optional, mzPeak C++ library) but slower (17:04); the archive's two-point TOF transform costs
    95	12% of identifications, so the loader recovers the exact TDF calibration from the embedded
    96	`vendor/analysis.tdf.gz` (or a `SPEXTRACT_MZPEAK_TDF=` sidecar) by default and fails closed without
    97	it (-1% vs `.d` then). `.d` remains the primary input.
    98	
    99	## The main open defect
   100	
   101	**Charge annotation.** Now **74.6%** with the current defaults (was 47.8%), against the reference implementation's
   102	**82.6%**. The catastrophic `4->2` confusion is gone. But **always answering z=2 would score
   103	69.6%** — so charge inference beats a constant by 5.0 points. It has stopped failing
   104	catastrophically rather than started working, and it remains the largest headroom in the tool.
   105	
   106	An MS1 funnel attempting to localise *where* precursors are lost was **retracted the same day it
   107	was produced** — see [docs/ms1-funnel.md](docs/ms1-funnel.md). A decoy floor added afterwards
   108	showed that a precursor which does not exist still matched an MS1 trace 91.9% of the time, so the
   109	assay measured trace density rather than correctness. **"97.8% detection ceiling" and "26.5% lost
   110	in inference" are withdrawn.** A tolerance sweep is running to find a usable operating point.
   111	
   112	The charge-agreement number does not depend on that assay and stands.
   113	
   114	This matters disproportionately for the intended use: dM = (z'-z)(m/z - 1.00728), so a z4->z2
   115	error at m/z 600 fabricates a -1198 Da phantom modification. A closed search hides this because
   116	the engine enumerates charge; an open search does not.
   117	
   118	**Run-to-run reproducibility is 0.17%** (4 runs of an identical effective configuration:
   119	8,411 / 8,408 / 8,406 / 8,397). Differences below ~0.2% are noise; the +46% delta is far above it.
   120	
   121	See [docs/](docs/) for the measurements, including levers that were **falsified** — every
   122	redundancy-reducing knob loses peptides at peptide-level FDR. One falsification has been
   123	**withdrawn**: ion mobility was retired against the wrong null (`max p_z` = 69.6% instead of
   124	`sum p_z^2` = 55.8%), so its true margin is **+19.2 points, not +5.4**. See
   125	[docs/charge-inference.md](docs/charge-inference.md).
   126	
   127	## Layout
   128	
   129	```
   130	src/        the tool
   131	scripts/    truth-set, funnel, FDR-recount and charge analyses
   132	docs/       benchmark results and architecture reviews
   133	```
   134	
   135	## License
   136	
   137	BSD-3-Clause. Derived from OpenMS (BSD-3-Clause) — see [NOTICE](NOTICE).
   138	
   139	## Charge inference: the two defaults are coupled, not independent
   140	
   141	Measured against a DIA-NN dataset B reference (`charge agreement` = our charge vs truth on matched
   142	spectra; always answering z=2 would score **69.6%**):
   143	
   144	| arm | charge agreement | `4->2` errors |
   145	|---|---|---|
   146	| ms1split + ambiguity_margin | **41.7%** | 34,554 |
   147	| ms1split alone | 45.8% | 19,995 |
   148	| baseline (`envelope`) | 49.7% | 10,199 |
   149	| `count` | 71.8% | -- |
   150	| **`ms1split` + `count`** (default) | **74.6%** | -- |
   151	
   152	**MS1 splitting must not ship without `count`** — an *empirical* coupling, not an architectural
   153	one. The two touch separate code paths (`trace:ms1_split_valleys` in `detectTraces_`,
   154	`charge:scoring` in `inferPrecursors_`); nothing in the code enforces the pairing, so the
   155	combination is a benchmark finding that a future edit could silently break. On its own MS1
   156	splitting drops charge agreement below baseline and
   157	more than triples the `4->2` confusion, which fabricates a ~-1198 Da phantom modification at
   158	m/z 600 -- invisible to closed search (the engine enumerates charge) and fatal to open search.
   159	Shipping `trace:ms1_split_valleys` without `charge:scoring count` would have been actively
   160	harmful for this tool's actual purpose.
   161	
   162	### `charge:ambiguity_margin` -- falsified, four tests
   163	
   164	| margin | peptide_q | vs `split_count` 8,411 |
   165	|---|---|---|
   166	| 0.5 | 8,408 | -0.04% |
   167	| 1.0 | 8,406 | -0.06% |
   168	| 2.0 | 8,397 | -0.2% |
   169	
   170	2.0 is the first value that can genuinely fire against an integer partner count (it admits any
   171	hypothesis within 2 partners of the best) and it still changes nothing. Hedging charge does not
   172	help; deciding it well does. Left at 0.

codex
The first pass already found a release-blocking pattern: several “fail closed” claims are not true on the optional mzPeak path. Missing batch frames and missing per-frame calibration data can be silently substituted or omitted, which is exactly the measurement-error class this tool says it is designed to prevent. I’m now checking every documented parameter/default and the reproducibility story against the executable path.
