     1	# dataset D is the decision file
     2	
     3	**All progress/regression decisions are made on dataset D alone.** Other samples are for confirmation
     4	once something has already won here — cross-sample comparison has produced three wrong
     5	conclusions in this project, and single-sample iteration removes that class of error entirely.
     6	
     7	## Reference numbers (frozen 2026-07-22)
     8	
     9	| measure | value | how |
    10	|---|---|---|
    11	| **peptides @ peptide_q ≤ 0.01** | **10,072** | `sage_deiso.json`, current defaults |
    12	| peptides, common-FDR procedure | 9,977 | DEPRECATED 2026-09-01 (procedure's primary outputs no longer exist) |
    13	| the reference implementation, same procedure | 11,552 | DEPRECATED (doc-only; canonical ref = 11,517) — legacy ratio 86.4% not citable |
    14	| MSFragger, same file | 10,470 | DEPRECATED (the reference implementation 13,014 doc-only; canonical = 13,932 form-level / 12,752 seq-level) |
    15	| PSMs/peptide | 8.98 | the reference implementation 1.66 |
    16	| within-cycle multiplicity | 2.85 | the defect |
    17	| across-cycle spread | 2.66 | **correct** — MS1 FWHM is 2.61 cycles |
    18	| MS1 FWHM | 3.61 s | flat with RT (1.04× across gradient) |
    19	
    20	**Current defaults:** `trace:ms1_split_valleys 7.0`, `charge:scoring count`, plus
    21	`ms2_chrom_peak_snr 1.0`, `ms2_noise_threshold_int 10`, `ms2_min_length_sec 0`,
    22	`ms2_split_valleys 7.0`.
    23	
    24	## Measurement methodology (2026-07-28) — variance model + estimator fix
    25	
    26	* **BOTH engines are DETERMINISTIC: run-to-run sigma = 0.** Re-searching one mzML (dataset D corr_power=2)
    27	  3x: Sage 10,802/10,802/10,802 AND MSFragger 11,560/11,560/11,560. So ALL benchmark peptide counts
    28	  are EXACT; n=1 is justified for both. CONSEQUENCE: the MSFragger k-curve non-monotonicity
    29	  (11,402/11,560/11,482/11,751 across k=1..4) is NOT run-to-run noise -- it is a REAL (non-smooth)
    30	  response to the different inputs (codex called this in review; now measured). The earlier "MSFragger
    31	  is a noisy guardrail" framing was WRONG on the noise: MSFragger numbers are exact, just non-monotonic
    32	  in k (its optimum k differs from Sage's). The ONLY stochastic uncertainty in the whole benchmark is
    33	  the entrapment FDR estimate (small n), which the bootstrap CI below now quantifies.
    34	* **Entrapment estimator was wrong TWO ways (fixed in `bench/entrapment.py`, codex #12):**
    35	  (1) it counted peptides SHARED between human and Arabidopsis as entrapment -> inflated the fraction
    36	  ~2.5x (dataset D base entrap 222 -> correctly 86 foreign-ONLY). (2) it used the PROTEIN ratio (0.800); the
    37	  correct PEPTIDE-hypothesis ratio (in-silico tryptic, Sage rules) is 0.6805, correction 1/r = 1.469.
    38	* **Corrected absolute FDR at nominal 1% peptide_q is ~1.0-1.45%, NOT the ~2.3% previously reported** --
    39	  the tool is WELL-CALIBRATED. With 95% bootstrap CIs (n_entrap ~ 60-90 -> noisy):
    40	  dataset D base 1.34% [1.06-1.65] / corr2 1.20% [0.95-1.46]; dataset A base 1.01% / corr2 1.08%; dataset B base 1.45%
    41	  [1.15-1.77] / corr2 1.18% [0.91-1.47]. **The arm FDR CIs OVERLAP** -> the earlier "FDR improved
    42	  2.33->2.13%" was an estimator+noise artifact. Correct statement: **corr_power adds +9-10% target
    43	  peptides at an entrapment FDR statistically INDISTINGUISHABLE from base** (more peptides, same FDR).
    44	* **Consequence for future levers:** the +1-3% regime (im_weight increments, cap sweeps, rescoring
    45	  features) needs this CI machinery + MSFragger replicate sigma to be falsifiable; do not decide small
    46	  levers on point estimates.
    47	
    48	## Decision rule
    49	
    50	* **Progress** = Sage peptides ≥ 10,802 (standard bench config = tool defaults + `-assembly:require_isotope_support`; re-based 2026-09-01, was 10,072 pre-gate/pre-corr_power) and PSMs/peptide not worse (guardrail retained).
    51	* **Regression** = peptides fall ≥ 1%.
    52	* **Noise floor** = 0.06% same-node (from four runs of an effectively identical config:
    53	  8,411 / 8,408 / 8,406 on one node). Differences below ~0.2% mean nothing.
    54	* **Screen on Sage** (4.2× faster, signs reproduce); confirm winners on MSFragger before any
    55	  default change. Sage is unreliable on effect SIZE — measured compression up to 3× on one arm.
    56	
    57	## Confirmed WINS post-freeze (gate baseline: Sage 9,989 / MSFragger 11,033 / entrap 9,304 @ 2.33%)
    58	
    59	* **`assembly:corr_power` (Q2) + `im_weight_sigma` STACK — WIN on BOTH engines, entrapment-confirmed (2026-07-27).**
    60	  Emitted fragment intensity *= co-elution^k. corr_power is EMIT-ONLY (excluded from the 500-cap key),
    61	  so the corr_power arms preserve peak MEMBERSHIP (identical 924,255 spectra + same peak m/z set; pure
    62	  intensity re-ranking). im_weight IS in the cap key, so with 73% of spectra at the 500-cap the STACK
    63	  also REORDERS which fragments survive (its intended interferent-removal channel) -- same spectrum +
    64	  peak COUNT, but not the same membership. So the stack's +310 over corr_power=2 has TWO channels
    65	  (intensity reweight + cap membership), not one [kimi 2026-07-27]. the reference implementation on dataset D: Sage 11,517,
    66	  MSFragger 13,932. MSFragger here is a NOISY guardrail (no replicate variance measured; screen on
    67	  Sage+entrapment). The 2.33->2.13% entrap frac is WITHIN Poisson noise (n~85 entrapment) -- a guardrail
    68	  (it passed: extra targets are not disproportionately entrapment), NOT an FDR win.
    69	  | arm | Sage | MSFragger | entrap target @ actual-FDR |
    70	  |---|---|---|---|
    71	  | base (gate) | 9,989 | 11,033 | 9,304 @ 2.33% |
    72	  | corr_power=1 | 10,622 | 11,402 | 9,894 @ 2.18% |
    73	  | corr_power=2 | 10,802 | 11,560 | 10,235 @ 2.21% |
    74	  | corr_power=3 | 10,901 | 11,482 | 10,288 @ 2.16% |
    75	  | corr_power=4 | 10,875 | 11,751 | 10,262 @ 2.15% |
    76	  | **corr_power=2 + im_weight 0.005** | **11,112 (96.5% of dt)** | **11,813 (84.8% of dt)** | **10,574 @ 2.13%** |
    77	  corr_power alone PEAKS at k≈3 on Sage; the im_weight STACK is best on EVERY metric incl. the LOWEST
    78	  entrap FDR (2.13%) -- the two are COMPLEMENTARY purity axes (RT co-elution vs IM), they ADD not erase.
    79	  Sage 86.7% -> 96.5% of the reference implementation; MSFragger 79.2% -> 84.8%. Mechanism: a true fragment co-elutes with
    80	  its precursor by construction (high c), so c^k down-weights mediocre-c INTERFERENTS. This OVERTURNED a
    81	  UNANIMOUS 3-CLI review that predicted -2..-5% MSFragger (premise "faint precursor -> low-c fragment"
    82	  was wrong). MSFragger is NOISY here (non-monotonic) -- screen on Sage+entrapment.
    83	
    84	  **GENERALISATION TEST PASSED for corr_power (frozen k=2/sigma=0.005 on untouched dataset A, dataset B; 2026-07-27):**
    85	  Sage nominal 1%: dataset A base 8,822 -> k2 9,735 (+10.4%) -> stack 10,428; dataset B base 8,311 -> k2 9,032 (+8.7%)
    86	  -> stack 9,159. So **corr_power=2 robustly generalises: +8-10% over base on ALL THREE files, both
    87	  engines (MSFragger dataset A +5.6%, dataset B +7.6%), at nominal 1% AND <=2% empirical FDR.** The im_weight STACK
    88	  increment is FILE-VARIABLE (+310 dataset D, +693 dataset A, +127 dataset B over k2; flat on MSFragger) -- a Sage-leaning
    89	  bonus, NOT a robust default increment (dataset B +127 < the pre-registered +200 threshold). The <=1% empirical
    90	  FDR numbers are erratic (k2 dips on dataset A, stack dips on dataset B) = estimator noise at ~50 entrapment n (the
    91	  <=2% and nominal metrics are clean). **DECISION (pending 3-CLI review of the result): ship corr_power=2
    92	  as the new default; im_weight_sigma=0.005 as a documented optional stack. Caveat: dataset A/dataset B are the same
    93	  study series as dataset D, so this de-risks the dataset D family, not diaPASEF broadly -- a different-cohort file is
    94	  the stronger (unavailable-today) test.**
    95	
    96	## What is already falsified on this file or dataset B
    97	
    98	Do not re-propose without new evidence: `competitive`, rank-pruning (`rp_max` 2/4/8),
    99	`assembly:apportion`, `trace:frame_aggregation_n` (3, 5), `charge:ambiguity_margin` (0.5/1.0/2.0
   100	— and it was *unreachable* in count mode, so it is untested rather than falsified),
   101	`consolidate:delta_rt` (0.7 → −12.7%, 3.0 → −24.0%), **`merge:rt_window` 1.4 → −4.4%**,
   102	mass recalibration, a monotonic ion-mobility charge prior, AlphaDIA-style log-sum co-elution
   103	scoring (three implementations), **`gate:variance_support` (Q1, union-support Pearson — 2026-07-27:
   104	DROP on unanimous 3-CLI analysis; it measures support co-LOCALISATION not co-elution SHAPE and reads
   105	faint fragments' censored shoulders as anti-correlation, e.g. precursor-8/frag-5/overlap-3 scores
   106	G-Pearson +0.47 but union −0.50. G-Pearson already ≈ robust cosine, so [B0] was cosmetic).**
   107	
   108	## Spectrum COLLAPSE (same precursor across cycles) — FALSIFIED for quality (2026-07-28)
   109	Tested on the OPEN-search entrapment results (simulation on existing PSMs, no re-search): group rank-1
   110	PSMs by precursor coordinate ACROSS RT (charge + expmass 10 ppm + IM 0.01, single-link chained over a
   111	10 s RT gap), keep one (or top-N) per group, RECOMPUTE the corrected entrapment FDR from scores.
   112	
   113	| rule | spx kept | reduction | targets @1% FDR | dt targets |
   114	|---|---|---|---|---|
   115	| full            | 558,255 | 1.00x | **8,273** | 9,743 |
   116	| apex (ID-agnostic) | 86,842 | 6.43x | 3,342 (-60%) | 4,491 (-54%) |
   117	| best (ORACLE)   | 86,842 | 6.43x | 4,390 (-47%) | 5,089 |
   118	| top3 / top5 / top10 (collapse + chimeric report_psms) | 132k/155k/190k | 4.2x/3.6x/2.9x | 5,448/5,767/6,603 | 6,068/6,672/7,422 |
   119	
   120	**The multiple-testing-burden hypothesis is REFUTED.** Every arm's FDR CI sits at ~0.6-1.3%: collapse
   121	removes true AND entrapment PSMs PROPORTIONALLY, so the FDR does not improve -- peptides are simply
   122	lost. Emitting fewer spectra does NOT buy open-search sensitivity.
   123	**It is INTRINSIC, not a speXtract defect:** the reference implementation collapses the same way (-24% at top10, -54% at
   124	apex) and BOTH tools reduce to the SAME ~87k precursor features (spx 86,842 / dt 87,112) -- speXtract
   125	just emits 1.33x more spectra per feature (6.43 vs 4.84). The across-cycle multiplicity is INDEPENDENT
   126	EVIDENCE (each cycle = a different noise realisation + co-isolation mixture, another shot at a different
   127	co-eluting peptide), not redundancy. Consistent with the long-standing "across-cycle spread 2.66 is
   128	CORRECT (MS1 FWHM = 2.61 cycles)" finding.
   129	**Residual option, untested:** TRUE merging (union of peaks -> better S/N) could in principle ADD IDs
   130	rather than select among existing ones; this simulation only bounds the SELECT-among-existing case. But
   131	it must beat a -20%..-60% deficit and it blends interferents across RT, so it is not promising.
   132	**Practical use:** collapse is a SPEED/recall knob, not a quality lever -- top10 gives 2.94x fewer
   133	spectra (much cheaper search) for -20% peptides, if total pipeline time ever matters more than recall.
   134	
   135	## Monoisotope -1.003 Da open-search artefact: STOPPING guard FALSIFIED, scoring fix under test (2026-07-28)
   136	The open-search re-baseline found our largest delta-mass artefact is a **-1.003 Da population** (top bin
   137	7,616 PSMs; 8.20% isotope-shifted vs the reference implementation 3.46%): the `charge:scoring=count` walk defines the
   138	monoisotope as the FURTHEST-LEFT peak it can reach, and `findPartner` matches m/z + RT + IM but **never
   139	intensity**, so it latches onto noise or a co-eluting species one isotope below the true mono. Closed
   140	search HIDES this (Sage enumerates `isotope_errors [-1,2]` and corrects it for free); open/blind search
   141	turns it into a phantom -1 Da modification.
   142	
   143	**Attempt 1 — `charge:mono_averagine_guard` (a STOPPING rule): FALSIFIED.** Require the leftward
   144	candidate to reach the averagine minimum 1/lambda times the previous peak. At slack 0.5 on dataset D:
   145	| metric | control | guard 0.5 |
   146	|---|---|---|
   147	| -1 Da bin | 7,616 | **2,734 (-64%, the target WORKED)** |
   148	| +1 Da bin | 4,697 | **23,994 (+411%)** |
   149	| total isotope-shift | 8.20% | **16.43% (WORSE)** |
   150	| open targets @ FDR | 8,887 @1.29% | 8,939 @1.28% (flat, CIs overlap) |
   151	| closed Sage | 10,802 | 11,697 (+8.3%) |
   152	| emission | 924,255 | 1,005,353 (+8.8%) |
   153	**A stopping rule can only trade over-reach for under-reach**, and worse: a walk that halts early leaves
   154	the M+1 peak unconsumed by `used[]`, so it later seeds its OWN precursor one isotope too heavy -- that is
   155	BOTH the +1 explosion and the +8.8% emission (one cause, two symptoms). Open search did not improve.
   156	NOTE the closed-search 11,697 (> the reference implementation 11,517) is NOT a quality win to bank: peptides/1k spectra is
   157	11.7 -> 11.6, i.e. the gain tracks the extra emission -- exactly the over-emission-rewards-closed-search
   158	corruption the reviewers warned about. Do not ship the guard for it.
   159	
   160	**Attempt 2 — `charge:mono_averagine_select` (a SCORING decision): under test.** Walk the full run, then
   161	choose WHICH peak is the mono by averagine cosine of the envelope starting there. Corrects BOTH
   162	directions, and every found peak is still consumed, so de-isotoping and emission stay unchanged (only the
   163	reported precursor mass moves) -- which also removes the emission confound from the experiment.
   164	
   165	### Attempt 2 result + the finding that STOPS this line of work (2026-07-29)
   166	`charge:mono_averagine_select` raw cosine repeated the envelope scorer's documented short-vector trap
   167	(`len` shrinks as the candidate mono moves right -> short vectors score high -> too-HEAVY mono). Adding
   168	the same evidence weight the envelope scorer uses, (len-1)/3 capped at 1, largely fixed that:
   169	| metric | control | guard 0.5 | msel raw | **msel + evidence weight** |
   170	|---|---|---|---|---|
   171	| emission | 924,255 | 1,005,353 | 924,716 | **924,552** |
   172	| -1 bin | 7,616 | 2,734 | 2,314 | **4,015 (-47%)** |
   173	| +1 bin | 4,697 | 23,994 | 21,591 | **12,188** |
   174	| unmod | 31.2% | 24.3% | 26.4% | **32.0% (best)** |
   175	| isotope-shift | **8.20%** | 16.43% | 15.24% | **9.48%** |
   176	| open targets @FDR | 8,887 @1.29% | 8,939 @1.28% | 8,916 @1.33% | 8,932 @1.33% |
   177	| closed Sage | 10,802 | 11,697 | 11,458 | 11,279 |
   178	Pre-registered criterion was total isotope-shift BELOW 8.20% with both +-1 bins down: **NOT MET (9.48%)**.
   179	Stopped rather than running a 4th variant. Both flags stay default-OFF, documented, for reproducibility.
   180	
   410	| **dataset B table model (shipping)** | 9,462 | 85 | 0.89 | **1.32** | 1.04-1.59 |
   411	| dataset B the reference implementation | 8,581 | 71 | 0.82 | 1.22 | 0.96-1.53 |
   412	The owed measurement (review F4) is now made: the shipping arm's true FDR at nominal 1% is ~1.4%, inside the
   413	same band as the vendor-calibrated arm (CIs overlap), so the open-path calibration gain is not bought with
   414	FDR. On all three files the shipping arm's true FDR at nominal 1% is 0.95-1.37%, inside the reference implementation's band on the two files where it exists (1.22-1.24%): the 3-file closed-search parity claim now carries its FDR control.
