   487	### C1(b) RESCORING A/B, like-for-like (2026-09-02, 21:46): rescoring does NOT close the MSFragger gap
   488	FragPipe 24.0 headless, Basic-Search (MSFragger 25 ppm, our human_decoy FASTA) -> Percolator -> Philosopher
   489	(sequential, picked, 1% peptide), MSBooster OFF for both (it aborts on our pepXML, see above):
   490	| dataset D arm | peptides @1% | PSMs @1% | PSMs / peptide | ratio spx/dt |
   491	|---|---|---|---|---|
   492	| speXtract apex | 13,211 | 113,685 | 8.6 | — |
   493	| the reference implementation | 15,947 | 31,700 | 2.0 | **82.8%** |
   494	| (raw-hyperscore walk, for reference) | 11,927 vs 13,932 | | | 85.6% |
   495	| (the reference implementation + MSBooster RT+spectra) | 18,670 | 37,086 | 2.0 | ours unavailable |
   496	Rescoring lifts both tools (+11% / +14%) and the ratio does not move (85.6 -> 82.8%). So the MSFragger deficit is
   497	not a scoring-function artefact that a rescorer repairs: the 3,600 the reference implementation-only peptides have no competitive PSM
   498	in our output at all. Together with H3 (identical scores on shared peptides) this points at CONTENT/coverage of the
   499	faint tail -- emission competition or missing fragments -- not at search-side handling. Our 8.6 PSMs per peptide vs
   500	the reference implementation's 2.0 is the over-emission signature: Percolator's peptide-level FDR gives redundancy nothing.
   501	**Decision (plan step 8):** the next attribution arms are content-side: (c) the emission-controlled arm (pre-registered
   502	step 02: quality-gate 924k -> ~700k + the reference implementation-precursor-matched sub-arm, both engines, full FDR curves) and (d) the
   503	oracle-fragment arm. The learned charge/mono predictor stays conditional on (d). A MSBooster-tolerant rerun (var mods
   504	capped identically for both tools) is owed for the +17% predictions bring the reference implementation.
   505	
   506	### STEP 02, sub-arm 1 -- PRE-REGISTERED (2026-09-02, 22:00): precursor-quality gate by isotope-envelope depth
   507	`SPEXTRACT_MIN_ISOTOPES=k` keeps precursors with >= k isotope peaks (k=2 is today's default via
   508	require_isotope_support; k=3 and k=4 are the arms). Emission falls with k; fragment sharing is untouched.
   509	Prediction if the MSFragger deficit is EMISSION COMPETITION: the spx/dt MSFragger ratio rises as emission falls
   510	toward the reference implementation's 700k (peptides@1% hold or rise while spectra drop). Prediction if it is FAINT-TAIL CONTENT: the
   511	ratio is flat or falls (the gate removes exactly the faint precursors). Gates: Sage set overlap vs the apex arm,
   512	MSFragger raw walk vs msf_dt, emission count; both engines before any default change.
   513	
   514	### STEP 02, sub-arm 2 -- PRE-REGISTERED (2026-09-02, 22:03): the reference implementation-precursor-MATCHED emission
   515	`SPEXTRACT_PRECURSOR_LIST=<rt_sec mz z>` keeps only our precursors that match a listed the reference implementation dataset D precursor
   516	(|dRT| <= 10 s, |dm/z| <= 10 ppm, same charge). The list is the reference implementation's own 700,434 pseudo-spectra (extracted from
   517	its mzML; RT converted from minutes). This holds the PRECURSOR POPULATION fixed and varies only the spectra.
   518	Prediction if the MSFragger deficit is EMISSION COMPETITION: with the same precursor set the spx/dt MSFragger ratio
   519	moves toward parity (>= 95%). Prediction if it is PER-PRECURSOR CONTENT (fragment coverage of the faint tail): the
   520	ratio stays near 85% even with matched precursors. Gates: emission count, Sage set vs the apex arm, MSFragger raw
   521	walk vs msf_dt. Runs after sub-arm 1 (`bench_match.sh`, d15_matched).
   522	
   523	### STEP 02 sub-arm 1 RESULT (2026-09-02, 22:34): EMISSION COMPETITION IS NOT THE MSFRAGGER MECHANISM
   524	| dataset D arm | precursors kept | spectra | Sage @1% (vs apex 12,537) | MSFragger @1% (vs dt 13,932) | ratio | wall |
