   697	engines but small (+1.5% / +0.6%); q=0.5 is much larger on MSFragger (+4.7%) at a −0.8% Sage cost. Per the both-engines
   698	rule neither is adopted on one file. **Pre-registered confirmation arm:** both settings on dataset A and dataset B, both engines,
   699	plus entrapment FDR. Adopt as default only if the dataset D sign holds on both files AND entrapment stays <= 1.4%
   700	(shipping arm: dataset D 1.37%, dataset A 0.95%, dataset B 1.32%); a Sage regression on dataset A/dataset B larger than dataset D's −0.8% kills q=0.5 and
   701	leaves K=150 as the only candidate.
   702	
   703	### ISOTOPE DUPLICATION: HYPOTHESIS (a) CONFIRMED, (b) REFUTED (2026-09-03, 09:35)
   704	Full analysis in [ISOTOPE-DUPLICATION-2026-09-03.md](ISOTOPE-DUPLICATION-2026-09-03.md). 31.4% of
   705	emitted spectra have a co-eluting same-charge partner 1-3 isotope steps below (decoy offset 3.8%,
   706	excess 27.7%); removing them leaves 671,270 spectra against the reference implementation's 700,434, i.e. isotope-offset
   707	duplication accounts for the entire emission excess. But they are NOT mergeable: content cosine
   708	0.505 vs 0.446 for an arbitrary co-eluting neighbour (only 6.5% above 0.9), and every id-agnostic
   709	collapse rule loses 5,011-5,173 of 14,944 peptides (oracle still loses 1,387). "Keep the lightest" is
   710	backwards -- where only the heavy member is identified, Sage says the mass we reported was already
   711	correct 70% of the time. Mechanism: `findPartner` skips `used[]` peaks, so a leftover one step above a
   712	consumed run becomes its own monoisotope (heavy members carry fewer isotope partners, 3.53 vs 4.59).
   713	Upstream ownership gate pre-registered; downstream merging stays falsified.
   714	
   715	### BACKFILL CONFIRMATION ARM: BOTH CANDIDATES FALSIFIED (2026-09-03, 11:47) -- backfill stays OFF
   716	Pre-registered arms `q=0.5` and `K=150` re-run on dataset A and dataset B, both engines, plus entrapment.
   717	| file | arm | Sage @1% | vs apex | MSFragger @1% | vs apex |
   718	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   719	| dataset D | apex | 12,537 | -- | 11,927 | -- |
   720	| dataset D | q=0.5 | 12,442 | −0.8% | 12,492 | +4.7% |
   721	| dataset D | K=150 | 12,724 | **+1.5%** | 12,000 | +0.6% |
   722	| dataset A | apex | 10,789 | -- | 11,444 | -- |
   723	| dataset A | q=0.5 | 10,516 | **−2.5%** | 11,620 | +1.5% |
   724	| dataset A | K=150 | 10,618 | **−1.6%** | 11,483 | +0.3% |
   725	| dataset B | apex | 10,156 | -- | 9,738 | -- |
   726	| dataset B | q=0.5 | 10,134 | −0.2% | 10,178 | +4.5% |
   727	| dataset B | K=150 | 10,166 | +0.1% | 9,751 | +0.1% |
   728	Entrapment is flat everywhere (dataset A apex 0.91% / q05 0.91% / K150 0.97%; dataset B 1.13% / 1.13% / 1.14%),
   729	so neither arm is bought with false positives -- but neither survives its own falsifier:
   730	* **K=150 dies on the sign rule.** dataset D said +1.5% on Sage; dataset A says −1.6%. The "strictly better on both
   731	  engines" claim was a one-file artefact.
   732	* **q=0.5 dies on the magnitude rule.** The pre-registration killed it if any dataset A/dataset B Sage regression
   733	  exceeded dataset D's −0.8%; dataset A is −2.5%. The MSFragger gain does replicate (+4.7 / +1.5 / +4.5), so the
   734	  two engines genuinely want different spectra -- but that makes it a per-engine option at best, not a
   735	  default, and nothing in the evidence says which engine to optimise.
   736	**Verdict: raw apex-frame backfill stays OFF by default in every form tested** (unconditional,
   737	fragment-gated, precursor-intensity-gated, empty-precursor). The MSFragger deficit is not closed by
   738	adding peaks to spectra we already emit.
   739	
   740	### NEW DEFAULT: `charge:min_charge` = 2 (2026-09-03, 15:05) -- confirmed on all three files, both engines
   741	Singly-charged precursor hypotheses are ~30% of emission and ~1.7% of peptides. A z=1 pseudo-spectrum
   742	is identified on 0.42% of its own spectra against 15.3% for z=2; tryptic peptides are essentially
   743	never 1+ in ESI and `charge:scoring count` breaks ties toward the LOW charge, so the mis-assignments
   744	land there. Dropping them removes their share of the multiple-testing burden too, so peptides go UP.
   745	| file | spectra | Sage @1% | MSFragger @1% | entrapment |
   746	|---|---|---|---|---|
   747	| dataset D | 927,813 -> **656,254** (−29.3%) | 12,537 -> **12,642** (+0.8%) | 11,927 -> **12,073** (+1.2%) | 1.28% -> 1.38% |
   748	| dataset A | 1,228,875 -> **863,319** (−29.7%) | 10,789 -> **11,084** (+2.7%) | 11,444 -> **12,061** (+5.4%) | 0.91% -> 0.99% |
   749	| dataset B | 844,755 -> **586,069** (−30.6%) | 10,156 -> **10,294** (+1.4%) | 9,738 -> **9,822** (+0.9%) | 1.13% -> 1.28% |
   750	dataset D wall time 15:21 -> 12:48 (−17%). Every pre-registered adoption condition is met: peptides rise on
   751	BOTH engines on ALL THREE files, every entrapment estimate stays inside the corresponding apex 95% CI
   752	and below the 1.4% bound. Against the reference implementation, MSFragger goes 85.6 -> 86.7% (dataset D), 83.7 -> 88.3% (dataset A),
   753	91.8 -> 92.6% (dataset B); Sage on dataset D is 109.8% of the reference implementation. This is the first change in the emission line
   754	that improves both engines while cutting emission AND runtime.
   755	**Adopted as the default.** `charge:min_charge=1` restores the old behaviour; 3+ is catastrophic
   756	(charge 2 carries 56.9% of peptides: dataset D Sage 5,088). The isotope collapse is NOT adopted in any form
   757	-- on top of this gate it turns +0.8% into −3.8% (Sage) and +1.2% into −2.5% (MSFragger).

