   264	## THE -8 ppm MASS BIAS: mechanism found + fixed (2026-09-01, follows the step-01 nearzero finding)
   265	**Every speXtract-reported m/z to date carries a systematic, m/z-dependent -5..-11 ppm error**
   266	(median -8.3 ppm on identified precursors; dt control -1.1 ppm on the same raw data).
   267	**Mechanism (proven by direct comparison against Bruker's timsdata library via ctypes):** the loader
   268	(BrukerTimsFile -> opentims++) converts TOF->m/z with `OpenSourceTof2MzConverter (linear-in-sqrt)` --
   269	a TWO-POINT chord fit to just (MzAcqRangeLower=100, MzAcqRangeUpper=1700, DigitizerNumSamples): error
   270	~0 at the anchors, bowing to -10.8 ppm mid-range, frame-independent -- reproducing every observed
   271	feature (flat in RT and mass-in-ppm; the "IM trend" is the m/z-dependence in disguise; temperature was
   272	a red herring, dC2=0 and dT1 tiny). The loader's SDK branch (OPENMS_BRUKER_SDK_PATH) upgraded ONLY the
   273	ion-mobility converter -- the m/z converter was never swapped (and the env var was never set anyway).
   274	**Fix: patches/openms-brukertims-sdk-mz.patch** -- 3 lines: the SDK branch now also installs
   275	`BrukerTof2MzConverterFactory` (vendor per-frame calibration via tims_index_to_mz). Requires
   276	OPENMS_BRUKER_SDK_PATH pointing at a local Bruker timsdata .so (present on the cluster from fp-tools;
   277	used locally, never redistributed; without it the tool falls back to the open model as before).
   278	The open-model inaccuracy is itself reportable upstream (opentims; also explains why mzdata disabled
   279	its own m/z model port).
   280	**Why mzPeak was NOT the answer (user directive "pull latest mzPeak", researched 2026-09-01):** spec
   281	is draft 0.9 / prototype v0.1.0 / breaking layout change 2026-07-14 / no releases; the current
   282	TDF->mzpeak converter stores RAW TOF indices and its own m/z path uses the same two-point model
   283	(mzdata implemented the true vendor model 0.66.3 but disabled it 0.66.5 as "not consistently better");
   284	the OpenMS in-tree MzPeakFile is unmerged/dormant and keyed to a superseded spelling. mzPeak re-poses
   285	the calibration problem; it does not solve it.
