   523	### STEP 02 sub-arm 1 RESULT (2026-09-02, 22:34): EMISSION COMPETITION IS NOT THE MSFRAGGER MECHANISM
   524	| dataset D arm | precursors kept | spectra | Sage @1% (vs apex 12,537) | MSFragger @1% (vs dt 13,932) | ratio | wall |
   525	|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   526	| apex (k=2, today's default) | 1,105,532 | 927,813 | 12,537 | 11,927 | 85.6% | 15:21 |
   527	| **k=3** (>= 2 isotope partners) | 655,297 | **566,537 (−39%)** | 11,739 (−6.4%; only-apex 1,010 / only-k3 212) | **11,820 (−0.9%)** | 84.8% | 11:53 |
   528	| k=4 | 399,837 | 353,689 (−62%) | 10,201 (−18.6%) | 10,538 (−11.6%) | 75.6% | 9:51 |
   529	Pre-registered reading: cutting emission by 39% -- to BELOW the reference implementation's 700k -- moves the MSFragger ratio from 85.6%
   530	to 84.8%. It does not rise. So the extra spectra are not what costs us on MSFragger (the emission-competition
   531	hypothesis is refuted for the second time, now with a precursor-quality gate rather than merging), and the gate removes
   532	real faint peptides on Sage (−6.4%). The deficit is per-precursor CONTENT of the faint tail, consistent with the
   533	rescoring A/B (ratio unchanged after Percolator) and H3 (identical scores on shared peptides). Sub-arm 2 (matched
   534	precursor population) is the direct test of that and is running. Side result: `SPEXTRACT_MIN_ISOTOPES=3` is a
   535	legitimate speed/emission knob (−39% spectra, −23% wall, −0.9% MSFragger, −6.4% Sage) -- not a default.
