   537	### STEP 02 sub-arm 2 RESULT (2026-09-02, 22:49): SAME PRECURSORS, SAME 85% -- the deficit is per-precursor content
   538	Reference: the reference implementation's 700,434 dataset D pseudo-spectra (RT/m/z/z). Of our 1,105,532 precursor hypotheses 445,008 match one
   539	(10 s / 10 ppm / z); emitting only those gives 432,579 spectra.
   540	| dataset D arm | spectra | Sage @1% | MSFragger @1% | MSFragger ratio vs dt |
   541	|---|---|---|---|---|
   542	| apex (all precursors) | 927,813 | 12,537 | 11,927 | 85.6% |
   543	| **the reference implementation-precursor-matched** | 432,579 | 11,903 (103% of dt's 11,517) | **11,842** | **85.0%** |
   544	| the reference implementation | 700,434 | 11,517 | 13,932 | — |
   545	With the precursor POPULATION held to the reference implementation's, the MSFragger ratio does not move (85.0%). Together with sub-arm 1
   546	(emission −39%: 84.8%), the rescoring A/B (Percolator: 82.8%) and H3 (identical scores on shared peptides), every
   547	search-side and population-side explanation is now excluded. What is left is the CONTENT of the spectra we emit for the
   548	precursors the reference implementation also emits: for ~3,600 peptides our spectrum of the same precursor does not reach MSFragger's
   549	1% threshold while the reference implementation's does, and on Sage it does. Candidates, in test order: (1) the intensity reshaping
   550	`corr_power=2` (a Sage win that MSFragger's rank-based hyperscore may dislike -- never measured on MSFragger; cp0 arm
   551	launched); (2) fragment coverage of the faint tail (our ~55 fragments vs the reference implementation's ~500 per spectrum, top-150 cap in
   552	MSFragger); (3) the oracle-fragment arm (true fragments at true charge/mono) to bound what content can give.
   553	Note also: only 62% of the reference implementation's emissions have a matching precursor of ours -- the other 38% are either duplicates
   554	on the reference implementation's side or precursors we never hypothesise (the MS1 funnel), a separate coverage question.
